system "DIN:krc-Cyrl:Latn:1460-2-2011-s7" {

metadata {
  authority_id din
  id 1460-2-2011-7
  language iso-639-3:krc
  source_script Cyrl
  destination_script Latn
  name "DIN 1460-2:2011-10, Tabelle 1 row 7: Balkarisch (Balkar) (Turkic)"
  url https://www.beuth.de/de/norm/din-1460-2/134564104
  creation_date 2011
  adoption_date
  description {
    DIN 1460-2:2011-10, Romanization of Cyrillic alphabets of
    non-Slavic languages, Tabelle 1, language row 7
    (Balkarisch (Balkar)). The table assigns each Cyrillic letter of the
    language's alphabet a Latin letter or letter group; the column
    order follows DIN 1460:1982 (Slavic). Extracted mechanically from
    the standard's text layer; the mapping rows below are the table's
    own cells.
  }

  notes {
    note "Extraction provenance: the PDF's text layer carries the full
    grid; columns were anchored on the per-page position-number row and
    each cell matched by x-coordinate to its Cyrillic header letter.
    Position 13's header glyph is PUA (U+E519) in the text layer and
    was visually identified at 10x as h with comma below (U+0068 + U+0322), the plain-h counterpart of position 12's hooktop-h symbol. Dialect qualifier: Balkar; the id carries the table's language number as the corpus table-suffix convention requires for shared ISO codes."
  }
}

tests {
   "a" ->  "a"
   "б" ->  "b"
   "в" ->  "v"
   "г" ->  "g"
# Exhaustive per-row tests for din-krc-Cyrl-Latn-1460-2-2011-s7, generated from the source
# document's table.
# Source: DIN 1460-2:2011-10, Tabelle 1, language row 7 (Balkarisch); held
# source
# interscript-private-references/din/din-1460-2.pdf. Rows are the language
# column's printed cells, taken from the durable mechanical extraction
# tools/din1460-2-table.json (x-anchored text-layer extraction, maps PR
# #242 provenance), re-verified cell-by-cell against the printed grid
# rectangles (pymupdf find_tables re-extraction, 2026-11-26) and
# end-to-end through the ts runtime (interscript-ts dist). Positions
# referenced by the table's own position numbers.
# Supplementary columns: the #242 extraction stops at position 90; the
# printed grid's columns 91-99 for this language are transcribed here from
# the grid (page 22): ю → ju, я → ja. Position 96's header is printed
# Cyrillic я; the text layer stores Latin 'ja' (the lookalike-glyph defect
# documented in the odni batch-1 artifacts).
# Findings (map-vs-source; rows present in this artifact but EXCLUDED from
# the generated tests, never forced green; record for the maps-fix
# pipeline): ю (position 95): printed 'ju', map outputs 'ю' | я (position
# 96): printed 'ja', map outputs 'я'
# Generated tests cover the 32 passing rows of 34; the map's own hand
